export const lsConcepts701800: any[] = [
  { id: "ls-nama-701", title: "Deśakālāparicchinnā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is not limited by time and space; She who is not measured by time and space. Literally deśa means place and kāla means time, while aparicchinnā means uncut and unbordered, so the name declares Her free of the two frames that bind every creature. The commentary aligns this with the Yoga Sūtra teaching that the Lord is untouched by time, and treats it as Her Brahman-credential: all beings live inside clocks and maps, while She contains them. Scripture then distinguishes Brahman with attributes, Śakti in action, from Brahman without attributes, Śiva as stillness, warning that the distinction is a teaching aid since one never functions without the other. In practice, sit still and notice the awareness in which time passes, resting as that witness rather than as a body with an appointment.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-137"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-702", title: "Sarvagā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who pervades all the worlds and all the living and non-living things; She who is omnipresent. The name follows directly from the last: once time and space no longer bind Her, the result is presence everywhere, another classic mark of Brahman. The commentary tells of Her asking Brahmā for omnipresence while doing penance, then anchors the claim in Upaniṣadic lines about the Lord dwelling both inside and outside all things, and in the Devī Māhātmya verse hailing Her as the embodiment of all beings and all powers. Being everywhere, She is also all-knowing and the very soul of creatures. In practice, pause wherever you are and feel Her simultaneously within the breath and beyond the horizon, refusing to confine Her to any shrine.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-137"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-703", title: "Sarvamohinī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who deludes all. Mohinī means the enchantress, and sarva extends the spell to everyone without exception, worked through the power called māyā. The commentary explains the puzzle: because She dwells in perishable and imperishable things alike, in opposites and contradictions, minds trying to grasp Her grow bewildered, and the Devī Māhātmya confesses that even the wise are drawn by this great enchantress. Kūrma Purāṇa calls this māyā infinite and dear to Śiva, while the Gītā observes that knowledge lies wrapped in ignorance so beings stumble into delusion, since māyā measures experience while Brahman stays immeasurable. She lifts the veil only for those advancing sincerely. In practice, when confusion strikes, stop arguing with appearances and pray to the Enchantress Herself to loosen the knot.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-137"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-704", title: "Sarasvatī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is in the form of knowledge. Sarasvatī, consort of Brahmā the creator, personifies the wisdom that dissolves the dyads imposed by māyā, since the Gītā describes knowledge as enveloped in ignorance and the universe can only be recognized as Her manifestation by the learned. The commentary distinguishes this functional form from attributeless Brahman: Lakṣmī and Sarasvatī fan the supreme Goddess as powers, yet here She is Sarasvatī Herself, one working face of the one reality, just as the Vedas parcel Brahman into gods and goddesses for worship. Tradition even honors a two-year-old girl as Sarasvatī and seats the goddess on every tongue as speech. In practice, study, teach, and speak truthfully, offering each clear sentence as Her worship and asking Her to uncover knowledge hidden by heedlessness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-137"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-705", title: "Śāstramayī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is in the form of the scriptures; She whose limbs are the scriptures. Śāstra means the authoritative teachings, and mayī means made of them through and through, so Her very body is revelation. The commentary cites the Chāndogya affirmation that all this is Brahman and the Brahma Sūtra principle that scripture is the source of Brahman-knowledge, since the Upaniṣads assert rather than merely infer, guiding by both affirmation and negation until the Pañcadaśī seal declares all as Śiva-Śakti. It then maps Her frame in detail: breath as Veda, ego as mantra, tongue birthing Sarasvatī, chin the Vedāṅgas, throat the sciences and sixty-four tantras, shoulders love itself. In practice, open scripture slowly and read it as Her limbs, letting each verse touch you like a hand.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-137"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-706", title: "Guhāmbā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the mother of guha (subramaṇya); She who dwells in the cave of the heart. Guhā carries two senses at once: the war-god Skanda, born of Śiva and Pārvatī to destroy demonic forces, whose mother She therefore is, and a hidden cave or concealment. The commentary draws the second sense from the Kaṭha Upaniṣad image of the Self and the self dwelling together in the cave of the intellect, tasting the fruits of action like light and shade, apparently two yet truly one. Thus the name places Her both on the battlefield of myth and in the secrecy of the heart. In practice, withdraw attention from outer noise for a few minutes daily and visit that inner cave, greeting the Mother who waits in darkness brighter than day.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-137"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-707", title: "Guhyarūpiṇī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who has a secret form. Guhya means hidden and rūpiṇī means embodied, so where the previous name lodged Her in a secret place, this one says secrecy is Her very shape. The commentary contrasts gross duality, which perishes inside time and space, with subtle non-duality, which outlasts them: mother-of-Guha is the outer form, while kāmakalā is subtler and kuṇḍalinī subtlest of all, like Her mantras and navāvaraṇa rites, all veiled by design. Kaṭha Upaniṣad promises that once the hidden Brahman is known, He hides no longer, though the Gītā warns such knowers are rarer than rare. In practice, keep one part of your worship entirely private and wordless, letting secrecy ripen sincerity until duality quietly falls away.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-137"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-708", title: "Sarvopādhivinirmuktā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is free from all limitations. Upādhi means a conditioning adjunct that makes the limitless look limited, like clear crystal seeming red beside a red flower, or consciousness seeming narrowed by intellect, knowledge, individuality, and māyā. The commentary explains that earlier names deliberately narrowed Her, as mother of this god or that scriptural function, to serve dualistic beginners who need form and ritual, while the knowledgeable approach non-dualistically; both paths confess one Brahman, differing only in method. This name then strips every adjunct away and reaffirms Her as nirguṇa Brahman beyond all conditions. In practice, notice each label you lay on yourself and on Her, such as role, mood, or image, and mentally set it down, resting in what remains when adjectives fall silent.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-138"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-709", title: "Sadāśivapativratā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is sadāśiva's devoted wife. Sadāśiva means the ever-blissful, counted among Śiva's five elemental faces as the supreme one presiding over re-creation after the great deluge, and as the thirty-fourth principle just below Śakti and Śiva in the thirty-sixfold map of awareness. The commentary reads His stage as the cry of aham-idam, I-am-here awaiting the union, where I-consciousness still glints with will before dissolving into Śakti and then Śiva, beyond whom nothing stands. Calling Her His devoted wife therefore pairs nirguṇa Brahman, attributeless Śiva, with saguṇa Brahman, His fully empowered consort who carries out creation, maintenance, and withdrawal. In practice, let devotion steady the restless I-thought until it softens into blissful readiness for the Self.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-138"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-710", title: "Sampradāyeśvarī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the guardian of sacred traditions. Sampradāya means knowledge handed down through custom, especially ritual lore passed from guru to disciple, and īśvarī means its sovereign mistress. The commentary stresses that high mantras like Pañcadaśī and Ṣoḍaśī require initiation by a realized teacher, warns against turning Śrīvidyā into busy public ritual instead of private devotion, and even prefers solitude over congregational display since everything about Her is secret. Where no true guru can be found, it permits rare self-initiation through Śiva or Viṣṇu as inner preceptor, provided meaning and procedure are understood. At a higher level, tradition itself is training wheels guiding beginners toward the Upaniṣadic realization of truth, knowledge, and infinity as Brahman. In practice, honor your lineage, keep worship quiet, and let outer form lead inward.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-138"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-711", title: "Sādhu", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who possesses equanimity. This edition counts sādhu and ī separately (711–712); other versions merge them as Sādhvī. The stotra line reads sādhvī, but since that word already occurred earlier, the compilers divide it into sādhu plus ī to avoid repetition, considered a blemish in so exact a hymn. Sādhu spans completing, accomplishing, overpowering, and understanding, and the commentary links it to Her parāhaṃtā, the supreme functional I-power by which the universe is sustained, since intellect and ego arise from the three guṇas and Brahman wears ego as a working sheath. Śiva stays still while Śakti acts, accomplishing ends and clearing ignorance, and sādhu also praises one who harms none and wills good for all. In practice, complete one duty cleanly today while deliberately harming nothing, mirroring Her benign efficacy.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-138"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-712", title: "Ī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the symbol Ī — the ī-kāra, the vowel of desire. This edition counts sādhu and ī separately (711–712); other versions merge them as Sādhvī. Ī names kāmakalā, Her subtle form shading into kuṇḍalinī as subtlest, and the commentary treats it as a potent creative seed: joined with the first vowel it figures Viṣṇu with His sister Śakti, while another reading divides Brahman into masculine and feminine lines meeting in this letter. Crowned with three dots it becomes Īṃ, a triangle evoking will, knowledge, and action and countless Upaniṣadic triads, a sound said to quicken Ṣoḍaśī practice and to concentrate the nectarine seed-syllables. In recitation it is voiced as yai. In practice, picture the luminous vowel kindling desire purified into creation, and guard it as one guards fire.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-138"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-713", title: "Gurūmaṇḍalarūpiṇī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who embodies in Herself the lineage of Gurus. Guru-maṇḍala means the circles of preceptors, and the name says their subtle lore, especially kāmakalā, must be learned mouth-to-ear rather than from books alone. The commentary notes that Śrīvidyā honors guru, paramaguru, and parameṣṭhiguru, worshipped with many others in navāvaraṇa, because custom and procedure differ between circles and the teacher's word settles practice. Ultimately there is no distance between preceptor and Goddess: the first guru is Śiva, the guru is adored in the sahasrāra where She joins Him, and to bow to the lineage is to bow to Her. In practice, remember your teachers by name before study or mantra, receive instruction as Her voice, and obey it with gratitude.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-138"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-714", title: "Kulottīrṇā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who transcends the senses. After nine earlier names exploring kula in many senses, here kula means the total apparatus of knowing, outer senses with the inner mind, and uttīrṇā means crossed beyond. The commentary assigns this transcendence to saguṇa Brahman in action, since attributeless Brahman merely witnesses, and names the active mode vimarśa, the self-reviewing power whose still ground is prakāśa, Śiva Himself. She uses the senses to govern the world yet is never confined by them. In practice, close the eyes, notice hearing, touch, and thought arising within a wider awareness, and rest briefly as that which beholds the senses rather than rushing out through them.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-139"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-715", title: "Bhagārādhyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is worshipped in the sun's disc. Bhaga here means the solar orb, so the name places Her worship in a region no body can enter, signaling secrecy and inwardness. The commentary recalls that She already stands at the sun's center, where Brahman alone is contemplated, and cites the Chāndogya vision of a golden person seen by seers within the disc. Read through kāmakalā, sun is one point of the triple fire-moon-sun emblem, while the union of the first vowel with ī yields e, a triangular letter hinting the central triangle of Śrīcakra where bindu-Śiva meets triangle-Śakti in the sahasrāra. In practice, visualize a small sun of awareness glowing in the heart, and lay your attention there as an oblation no stranger can see.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-139"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-716", title: "Māyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is illusion. The root mā means to measure, so māyā is the power by which the immeasurable Brahman appears measurable, seemingly bound by the time and space it transcends. The commentary explains that attributeless Brahman joined to māyā appears as Brahman with attributes, projecting the universe the way pure consciousness, subtlest of existents, seems diversified by superimposed names and forms that are neither simply real nor unreal. This self-veiling sheath breeds ignorance until knowledge removes it, and since Śiva is Brahman while Śakti is māyā, only She can clear the road to Him by cleansing gross, subtle, and causal impurities, a crossing the Gītā calls arduous yet possible through devotion. In practice, when the world feels solid and separate, surrender the measuring mind to Her and ask for clearer sight.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-139"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-717", title: "Madhumatī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She whose nature is as sweet as honey. Madhu means honey and matī means intellect and judgment, together suggesting wisdom refined to sweetness. The commentary draws on the Chāndogya madhu-vidyā, where the sun is called honey of the gods because it concentrates all auspicious human effort, just as bees gather widely and the hive yields one essence, an image for supreme knowledge distilled from many sources. It also recalls the sevenfold knowledge in Yoga, whose final stage bears this very name, marking complete understanding with settled contentment. Gross learning is the flower, realization the honey. In practice, gather experience widely but distill nightly: ask what single sweet insight the day yielded, and store it like honey for harder seasons.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-139"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-718", title: "Mahī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the goddess earth. After long praise of Her subtle forms, the hymn suddenly names the visible ground itself, since earth can be seen by eyes unable yet to contemplate abstractions. The commentary reads this through the pair of principles, unconscious primordial matter and the conscious person, understanding the planet as Her gross embodiment for beginners. Those unready for kāmakalā or kuṇḍalinī, which demand highly gathered mind, may begin with soil, grain, and mountain. Subtlety remains the goal, but condescension is grace. In practice, touch the ground with attention before meditation, thank the earth that bears your seat, and see one familiar landscape as Her body offered for the slow of sight.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-139"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-719", title: "Gaṇāmbā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the mother of shiva's attendants. Gaṇas are the troops of attendants serving Śiva under Gaṇeśa, also called Gaṇapati as their chief, and ambā means mother, so the name enthrones Her over that whole host. The commentary adds that Gaṇa can also directly mean Gaṇeśa Himself, making Her His mother too, the source of the remover of obstacles who commands the ranks. To call Her mother of warriors is to promise protection conducted in good order rather than by chance. In practice, begin undertakings by honoring Gaṇeśa and the unseen helpers, asking the Mother of the host to post Her guardians around your work and your household.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-139"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-720", title: "Guhyakārādhyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is worshipped by guhyakas (a kind of devās). Guhyakas are the hidden troops of Kubera, lord of wealth and a devoted servant of the Goddess, himself counted among the seers of Pañcadaśī. The commentary therefore reads the name as praise of Her lofty worshippers, then turns it inward: guhya also means secret and ārādhya means worthy of worship, so She is adored in the secret station of mūlādhāra as kuṇḍalinī, the subtlest form reserved for interior rite. Wealth guards Her, yet She hides deeper than wealth. In practice, dedicate one brief daily attention to the base of the spine, breathing quietly there as though laying a flower where no eye follows.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-139"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-721", title: "Komalāṅgī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who has beautiful limbs. Komala means soft and tender, and aṅgī praises every limb, a wonder since the same Goddess discharges the stern cosmic offices of creating, preserving, and dissolving, and has overthrown countless demonic forces at Śiva's charge. The commentary insists the tenderness is mental as well as bodily: precisely because She is soft, She bends toward devotees instead of breaking them, yielding like a young shoot rather than resisting like iron. Some read here a hint of kuṇḍalinī, finest of all forms. In practice, approach Her gently rather than with strained effort, softening posture, voice, and demand, and trust that suppleness in you will answer suppleness in Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-139"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-722", title: "Gurupriyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is beloved of the gurus. Guru first of all means Śiva, who taught Her the entire tantra lore, so as His wife She is g Patnī, the preceptor's consort, owed the same reverence as the preceptor himself, if not more. The commentary extends the love to human gurus who transmit Śrīvidyā initiation in mantras like Pañcadaśī and Ṣoḍaśī, and even to Bṛhaspati, preceptor of the gods as Venus is of the demons, recalling earlier names that call Her Herself the guru and the guru-circle. Love circulates between teaching and embodiment. In practice, thank a teacher who shaped you, support those who preserve living instruction, and receive correction as a form of Her affection.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-139"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-723", title: "Svatantrā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is free from all limitations. Sva points to the Self and supreme authority, Śiva, while tantra means both sacred treatise and dependence, so the name simultaneously calls Her the embodiment of the sixty-four treatises and the one whose dependence on Śiva is mutual. The commentary recalls that tantric practice bears no fruit without invoking Her, cites the Kālikā Purāṇa image of Śiva sporting forever with His Śakti, and crowns the idea with svātantrya, the Absolute's unobstructed will, transferred as it were by power of attorney from prakāśa-Śiva to vimarśa-Śakti for the work of the worlds. Freedom here is not isolation but entrusted omnipotence. In practice, exercise one choice today as a trustee rather than an owner, asking whether its use serves the whole that lends you will.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-140"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-724", title: "Sarvatantreśī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the goddess of all tantras. Sarva means all, tantra the revealed ritual sciences, and īśvarī their sovereign, so where the last name called Her free, this one calls Her foundational. The commentary explains that every tantric undertaking rests on Her and ripens only when She is invoked, whether the rite follows Vedic or left-hand-leaning Śrīvidyā procedure. She is not one deity among the systems but the common ground on which their differences stand. In practice, before any formal practice, acknowledge Her as the source of method itself, dedicating the fruits of technique to understanding rather than to display, and let reverence precede precision.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-140"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-725", title: "Dakṣiṇāmūrtirūpiṇī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is in the form of dakṣiṇāmūrti. Dakṣiṇāmūrti is Śiva as the south-facing silent teacher, and the name identifies Her with that very guru-form, a paradox the commentary savors by dividing the word as skilful formlessness. It recalls how Śiva, bereaved after His consort's self-offering, sat facing south in meditation until the aged mind-born sages Sanaka and his brothers begged for Self-knowledge, which He conveyed without speech through the joining of thumb and forefinger, signifying soul and Brahman made one. His hymn by Śaṅkara compresses the whole path, and He stands as seer of the great Ṣoḍaśī mantra. In practice, spend a few minutes in deliberate silence before a teacher or text, letting stillness itself instruct beyond words.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-140"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-726", title: "Sanakādisamārādhyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is worshipped by sanaka and other sages. Sanaka, Sana, Sanatkumāra, and Sanandana are the mind-born sons of Brahmā who sat as aged disciples before the ever-young silent teacher beneath the great banyan, itself an emblem of the macrocosm unfolding from a tiny seed as the gross grows from the subtle. The commentary stresses that their initiation passed without exchange of words, marks them as great devotees of the Goddess, and recalls the Chāndogya praise of Sanatkumāra leading Nārada beyond darkness into perfect knowledge. To be adored by such knowers is proof of supreme rank. In practice, imitate their receptivity: sit low, grow quiet, and let a single profound teaching work in you like a seed rather than chasing many teachings.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-140"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-727", title: "Śivajñānapradāyinī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who bestows the knowledge of siva. Śiva-jñāna means knowledge of the ultimate Brahman, and the name declares that She alone confers it, since Śiva is reached only through Śakti the way wind is known by movement and fire by heat. The commentary gathers witnesses: scripture calls Śiva pure consciousness and sovereign will in knowing and acting, the Śiva Sūtra opens with consciousness as Self and promises that meditation on Śakti dissolves the universe as separate, while Spanda describes the bound one entering the energetic circle of Self and becoming equal to it. Without Her choice, the inaccessible stays inaccessible. In practice, when longing for the Absolute, first turn to the Mother with devotion, asking Her to make the mind an offering fit for Śiva.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-140"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-728", title: "Citkalā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the consciousness in brahman. Cit means foundational awareness itself, while kalā, distinct from time, means the power by which awareness seems limited in activity, slightly below māyā among the five coverings. The commentary lists Her five great powers as consciousness, bliss, will, knowledge, and action, explaining how first-person Śiva appears as second-person Śakti and third-person humanity when cit is curtained by minute sheaths, a bondage Spanda blames on glory stolen by kalā amid the multitude of words. Yet the Gītā remembers the soul as an eternal fragment of the Lord, so Her presence in the body is precisely this cit-kalā. In practice, pause and notice bare awareness before thoughts dress it, honoring that glint as Her foothold within you.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-141"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-729", title: "Ānandakalikā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the bud of bliss. Ānanda means bliss and kalikā means a bud, so the name pictures joy not yet fully opened, the stage reached when knowledge has begun clearing the sheaths described before. The commentary distinguishes this from ordinary happiness by recalling the Taittirīya and Bṛhadāraṇyaka architectures of bliss, multiplying human joys a hundredfold until the particle sustaining all beings is revealed as supreme, with Brahman shining as consciousness wedded to will, knowledge, and action. Devotion establishes the living link by which She ripens the bud at the right season into union with Śiva. In practice, protect small quiet joys instead of spending them loudly, keeping one unopened delight before Her until it flowers in its own time.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-141"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-730", title: "Premarūpā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is pure love. Prema means love in its selfless fullness, and rūpā means Her very form, so the name presents affection not as something She occasionally shows but as what She is, the natural temper of the Supreme Mother. The commentary is brief because the point is simple: from such an embodied source, love must emanate the way light leaves a lamp, touching children before argument begins. Sovereignty and metaphysics gather here into tenderness. In practice, let one interaction today be governed solely by maternal goodwill, offering patience without calculating return, and recognize the warmth that moves through you as Her form briefly wearing your face.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-141"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-731", title: "Priyaṅkarī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who grants what is dear to Her devotees. Priya means beloved and karī means maker, so She both awakens love in human hearts and bestows the things they love. The commentary reasons by emanation: the sun cannot lend light unless full of light, nor can She kindle love unless love overflows from Her. It then sorts human longings into two baskets, material prosperity for the unawakened and liberation for the knower, giving each what is sought, so the gift reveals the seeker. In practice, examine one strong wish to see which basket holds it, purify the motive if you can, and lay the wish before Her, accepting either fulfillment or redirection as instruction.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-141"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-732", title: "Nāmapārāyaṇaprītā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is pleased by the repetition of Her names. Pārāyaṇa means devoted repeated recitation, and the name says such circling through Her thousand names delights Her, recalling how Lalitā Herself commissioned the Vāgdevīs to compose the hymn and rejoiced when they sang it, with the closing chapter prescribing its fruits. The commentary widens the term from this liturgy to ghaṭikā recitation, reckoning the Sanskrit letters from a to kṣa, charged with principles as mother-glyphs, into tens of thousands of combinations to be completed across day, week, month, season, or year. Quantity serves constancy. In practice, fix a small daily count of Her names and keep it without break, letting repetition polish attention the way treading polishes a path.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-141"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-733", title: "Nandividyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the deity worshipped by the nandi mantra. Nandi-vidyā points to the lore served by Nandi, counted among the seers of Śrīvidyā beyond the famous twelve, though tradition debates whether he is Śiva's bull-vehicle, a sage, or Śiva Himself, whom the Tamil saint Tirumūlar addresses as Nandi and who also stands among Pañcadaśī seers. The commentary leaves the identity open, content that so intimate a servant tends Her mantra, usually entered under Ānanda Bhairava as seer. Mystery of authorship does not dim efficacy of lineage. In practice, honor the unnamed servants who carried wisdom to you, keeping one practice exactly as taught, and let fidelity to transmission be your offering to the Goddess they serve.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-141"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-734", title: "Naṭeśvarī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the wife of natesha (shiva). Naṭeśa means lord of dance, Śiva as Naṭarāja, so Naṭeśvarī is His partner in the cosmic performance. The commentary cites Saundarya Laharī on the great dance of nine aesthetic moods, distinguishing vigorous tāṇḍava proper to Him from graceful nāṭya proper to Her while keeping them one household art, and even preserves old Tamil syllables sounded at His blissful dance, suggesting rhythm itself is theology. To marry the dancer is to wed motion and stillness together. In practice, offer Her a measured art today, a song, step, or even orderly work done rhythmically, remembering that the universe keeps time because this couple keeps dancing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-141"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-735", title: "Mithyājagadadhiṣṭhānā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the basis of the illusory universe. Mithyā means seemingly real yet finally false, jagat the moving world, and adhiṣṭhāna the ground supporting it, so She underlies the very show that hides Her, wielding māyā as prop. The commentary opens with the Bhāgavata meditation on the reality from which the three-stranded creation appears real though unreal, then cites Bṛhadāraṇyaka counsel to see no diversity in Brahman on pain of passing from death to death, since duality is superimposed ignorance. Vedānta seals it with clay and pot: cause and effect are one substance, and to call the ground illusory would divorce universe from Brahman. In practice, when multiplicity agitates you, trace pot back to clay, asking what single ground supports these many forms, and rest upon Her in quiet trust.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-142"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-736", title: "Muktidā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who gives liberation. Mukti means release from transmigration, and dā means giver, so the name enthrones Her as bestower of the highest gift, though strictly within the law of karma rather than by overriding it. The commentary insists that worthiness matters and that liberation comes only through Her, since She alone can conduct the soul to Śiva, a theme the preceding name on Śiva-knowledge prepared and the next name on liberation-as-form completes. Grace respects order while fulfilling it. In practice, stop bargaining for shortcuts and instead clean one corner of conduct, then ask Her to grant freedom when the account is ripe, trusting Her timing over your urgency.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-142"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-737", title: "Muktirūpiṇī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is in the form of liberation. Where the last name called Her giver, this one calls Her the gift itself, since only what overflows can be shared. The commentary explains that knowledge alone, though necessary, does not finish the work: ignorance removed ends bondage, but liberation dawns in abiding bliss when the karmic account permits, the fifth stage called kaivalya by Sāṅkhya-Yoga and mokṣa by Advaitins. It further distinguishes temporary rest in high worlds from final merger with Brahman beyond return, pairing these two names the way love and its kindling were paired before. In practice, do not merely collect insights but savor one quiet moment of contentment as Her form, letting bliss seal knowledge into freedom.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-142"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-738", title: "Lāsyapriyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is fond of the lāsya dance. Lāsya means the graceful dance of women, moving with instrumental music and song, in contrast to the vigorous tāṇḍava, and priyā means She loves it. The commentary is content to record Her delight in such refined entertainment, noting that the Lalitā Triśatī twice celebrates Her taste for lāsya, as though delicacy itself were a divine attribute rather than mere pastime. Power here wears anklets instead of armor, and sovereignty expresses itself through rhythm. In practice, cultivate one graceful art of movement, speech, or gesture, performing it attentively as an offering, and let elegance become a small daily temple where She is pleased to linger.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-142"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-739", title: "Layakarī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who causes absorption. Laya carries a musical sense, the balancing of leading and supporting rhythm whose mother is laya while father is tone, and a yogic sense, interiorization where consciousness dissolves past meditation into the Brahman. The commentary holds both: She keeps the universe livable by proportioning its harmonies, and the Śiva Sūtra teaching that the world is His energy expanding into impressions and dissolving back explains why total mental absorption alone reveals reality. Karī means She causes this merger. In practice, follow one sound, breath, or mantra until accompaniments fall away and only listening remains, asking Her who mothers rhythm to steady you into the silence beneath music.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-142"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-740", title: "Lajjā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who exists as modesty in living beings. Lajjā means bashfulness, counted among the natural graces of womanhood, and the name says She dwells in creatures as that reticence. The commentary identifies hrīṃ as the lajjā-bīja, also called māyā-bīja, and pictures Her as shy before pompous display, caring less for material splendor than for what is offered from within. It compares Her true devotee to a tortoise drawing limbs inward, distinguishes shared celebration from conceited ritual, and urges worship in solitude. In practice, simplify one offering until only sincerity remains, praying quietly without audience, and let modesty guard devotion the way a shell guards life.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-142"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-741", title: "Rambhādivanditā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is adored by the celestial damsels such as rambhā. Rambhā, Urvaśī, and their companions are the famed beauties of heaven, and vanditā means worshipped by them, implying a loveliness before which even loveliness bows. The commentary notes that Rambhā can also name Gaurī Herself, deepening the hint, and treats this adoration as prelude to the eight following names in which the Goddess turns destroyer of evils so that souls may reach Śiva. Beauty salutes before battle. In practice, when you meet exceptional beauty in person or nature, refuse envy and instead trace it upward, saluting the source whose nymphs themselves worship, and ask that your admiration become prayer.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-142"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-742", title: "Bhavadāvasudhāvṛṣṭiḥ", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the rain of nectar falling on the forest fire of worldly existence. Bhava means transmigratory life, dhāva a forest blaze, sudhā nectar, and vṛṣṭi a downpour, so the image shows saṃsāra burning like dry woods while She quenches it with knowledge-rain, since ignorance feeds indulgence and wisdom starves it. The commentary also dissects the compound as giver of Śiva and giver of wealth, promising sincere devotees both liberation and sustenance, while warning that the ignorant merely use the shower to make the forest grow thicker under māyā. Only the discerning transcend. In practice, name one craving that scorches you and deliberately pour cool reflection upon it, begging Her rain to fall where your private fire burns hottest.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-143"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-743", title: "Pāpāraṇyadavānalā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is like wild fire to the forest of sins. Pāpa means sin, araṇya forest, and davānala the blaze that consumes it, reversing the last image: there She was rain to fire, here She is fire to forest. The commentary pairs Her with the earlier sin-destroyer, cites Rudra Yāmala counsel to repeat Pañcadaśī a thousand and eight times standing in water with faith, recalls Triśatī parallels, and seals the promise with the Gītā assurance that refuge in the Lord absolves all sins. Severity here is mercy in work clothes. In practice, bring one persistent fault into the open before Her, repeat Her mantra with contrition, and let Her heat do what your resolutions alone cannot burn away.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-143"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-744", title: "Daurbhāgyatūlavātūlā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the gale that drives away the cotton wisps of misfortune. Daurbhāgya means ill fortune, tūla cotton, and vātūlā a whirlwind, picturing karmas heaped over lifetimes like a huge bale of fluff that Her grace scatters. The commentary cautions against magic thinking: She does not simply cancel the moral ledger but moves sinners to perform counterbalancing good, thinning misery's intensity while honoring causality. To show the exit from evil while respecting law is itself compassion. In practice, answer one old wrong with a deliberate good deed, however small, and picture Her wind catching the cotton of your past, lifting it grain by grain until the sky clears.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-143"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-745", title: "Jarādhvāntaraviprabhā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the sunlight that dispels the darkness of old age. Jarā means aging, dhvānta darkness, ravi the sun, and prabhā the dawn of illumination, so the name promises daybreak against the gloom of decay. The commentary locates the darkness in mistaking body for self: the frame withers while the ātman endures, soul and then Brahman to be understood in turn, and clinging to perishables is the ignorance She burns away. It recalls the Gītā image of casting worn garments for new ones, urging devotees not to dread aging or death but to transfer allegiance to the undying. In practice, when a mirror or ache reminds you of time, greet the imperishable guest within the aging house, and let Her sunrise fall on fear.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-143"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-746", title: "Bhāgyābdhicandrikā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the full moon to the ocean of good fortune. Bhāgya means fortune, abdhi ocean, and candrikā moonlight, so where the last name gave sunlight against age, this one gives moonbeams that swell prosperity the way the waxing moon lifts tides to their full-moon height. The commentary delights in the pairing, remembering that sun and moon are Her two eyes, and reads the verse poetically: at Her sight, fortunes rise like waters answering the moon. Grace has phases, and fullness answers fullness. In practice, turn toward Her when undertaking anything hopeful, imagining cool bright attention drawing your shrunken purposes upward like a tide, and proceed while the moon of Her regard is full.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-144"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-747", title: "Bhaktacittakekighanāghanā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the cloud that gladdens the peacocks who are the hearts of Her devotees. Devotees' minds are peacocks, She the dark rain-bearing cloud, and ghanāghanā redoubles the cloud to stress unbroken continuity of grace, since peacocks dance and cry keka at thunderheads. The commentary, echoing Śivānanda Laharī where Pārvatī-clouds cross Śiva's sky-hair, insists that seeing here means abiding inner connection through fixed higher awareness, not physical sight, and joins this name with the last to commend mind over body after bodily prestige was renounced. In practice, visualize Her as monsoon darkness gathering above the heart, let attention spread its feathers and dance in expectation, and receive each drop of remembrance as rain.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-144"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-748", title: "Rogaparvatadambholiḥ", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the thunderbolt that shatters the mountain of disease. Roga means sickness, parvata mountain, and dambholi the thunderbolt, Indra's diamond weapon that splits even cliffs, so no ailment is too massive for Her stroke. The commentary reads the image without qualification as assurance that She cures Her devotees' diseases, pairing medical realism with divine aid rather than replacing one by the other. Lightning does in an instant what erosion needs ages to achieve. In practice, tend the body diligently with remedy, rest, and counsel, while also laying the mountain of your illness before Her, asking the bolt of Her attention to crack what habit and medicine alone cannot move.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-144"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-749", title: "Mṛtyudārukuṭhārikā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the axe that cuts down the tree of death. Mṛtyu means death, dāru wood, and kuṭhārikā the axe, so mortality itself is timber before Her blade. The commentary cites Kaṭha Upaniṣad on death as mere seasoning to the Self, names dread of dying as humanity's worst fear, and traces life's twenty-eight pains to nescience, ego, craving, anger, and clinging, all fuel for that tree. Faith in Her does not always postpone the fall but removes its terror. In practice, contemplate one small ending each evening as rehearsal, entrusting the unknown hour to the Mother with the axe, and sleep like one already guarded against the final felling.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-144"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-750", title: "Maheśvarī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the supreme goddess. Maheśvara means the Great Lord Śiva, and Maheśvarī, His feminine counterpart, carries greatness into every syllable, distinct in stress from the earlier Māheśvarī yet converging on supremacy. The commentary is deliberately terse, as if majesty needed no argument: to be His wife in this register is to share His eminence over all orders of being. After axes and thunderbolts, the hymn pauses to salute rank itself. In practice, address Her once today with full titular reverence, straightening posture and speech, and let the acknowledgment of Her supremacy quietly demote every lesser sovereignty clamoring for your allegiance.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-145"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-751", title: "Mahākālī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the great kāli. Mahat marks supremacy and intellect alike, second only to prakṛti and puruṣa in Sāṅkhya reckoning, while Kālī spans blackness, cloud, dark-complexioned woman, Vyāsa's mother, Sarasvatī in one mood, and Durgā in another. The commentary gathers Purāṇic births of blue-necked Kālī from poison and from Śiva's brow-fire, identifies Mahākāla-Śiva with Mahākālī-Śakti, and understands Her darkness as the hue She assumes to dissolve evildoers and to govern death itself, a compassion the Kanchi sage compared to sleep: tamas granting exhausted runners nightly peace, and dissolution granting longer rest from karma until the egoless wake no more. In practice, surrender one gripping attachment into Her darkness, trusting blackness to be not absence but rest.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-145"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-752", title: "Mahāgrāsā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who devours everything great; She who is the great devourer. Grāsā means a voracious mouthful, and mahā enlarges both eater and eaten, so the name pictures the universe returning into Brahman at the great dissolution. The commentary summons Kaṭha Upaniṣad where even the foremost mortals are food to the Self and death itself a condiment, alongside the Brahma Sūtra ruling that the Eater appropriates all moving and unmoving things. Appetite here is eschatology wearing a homely mask. In practice, offer back one thing you clutch, a plan, praise, or possession, imagining it dissolving into a vaster hunger that wastes nothing, and feel relief where ownership once ached.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-145"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-753", title: "Mahāśanā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who eats everything that is great. Nearly twin to the last name, this one shifts the emphasis from universal dissolution to selective destruction: where the great devouring swallowed the whole at deluge, this great eating consumes the corrupt part, the evildoer rather than the cosmos. The commentary is careful to separate the pair on exactly this ground and to distinguish the word from the similar-looking seat-name elsewhere, showing how fine the hymn's teeth are ground. Providence both clears the field and keeps the harvest. In practice, invite Her to consume one festering habit while sparing the life around it, like a surgeon rather than a flood, and cooperate by starving what you asked Her to eat.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-145"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-754", title: "Aparṇā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who owes no debt. With the negative prefix set against ṛṇa, obligation, the name declares Her indebted to none: She repays devotees' love with blessings, serves Śiva by managing His creation, and rescues the gods from demons, balancing every ledger. The commentary tells of Bhāskararāya borrowing for Her rites until creditors pressed, when the Goddess Herself, in his wife's form, discharged the loans, proving wealth and unfeigned devotion seldom travel together. Aparṇā also means leafless, recalling Pārvatī's fast without even leaves to win Śiva, and by another cut means without fall, since Brahman never lapses. In practice, settle one debt promptly, whether money, thanks, or apology, and emulate the Mother who leaves no account open.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-145"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-755", title: "Caṇḍikā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is angry (at the wicked). Caṇḍikā or Caṇḍī, also honored as a seven-year-old girl, names Durgā grown formidable, the combined force of Durgā, Lakṣmī, and Sarasvatī turned against evildoers without mercy. The commentary finds warrant in the Taittirīya vision of wind, sun, fire, Indra, and death all hastening from fear of Brahman, and in Śrī Rudram opening with homage to His wrath, concluding that Her anger is the discipline by which cosmic business keeps its hours. Terror here is administration. In practice, let reverent fear tidy one slack duty, imagining Her frown upon cruelty and sloth, and keep your own anger reserved strictly for injustice rather than convenience.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-145"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-756", title: "Caṇḍamuṇḍāsuraniṣūdinī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who killed caṇḍa, muṇḍa and other asuras. Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa were formidable demons whose slaughter won the slayer the title Cāmuṇḍā, woven from their initials, as the Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa records the Goddess bestowing the name on Kālī. The commentary distinguishes Caṇḍikā, the triple-formed Durgā-Lakṣmī-Sarasvatī majesty, from Cāmuṇḍā, the Kālī execution-form among the seven mothers, while insisting both are faces of Lalitā proving that anger at evil is policy, not temper. Doctrine becomes biography. In practice, name two brutish tendencies in yourself without euphemism, lay them before Cāmuṇḍā like captives, and ask that what myth accomplished in demons be accomplished in your habits.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-145"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-757", title: "Kṣarākṣarātmikā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is in the form of both the perishable and imperishable Atman. The compound bears two great readings. In one, kṣara are syllables and akṣara the letters composing them, making Her mono-syllable and multi-syllable at once, mistress of the mother-letters from a to kṣa, the supreme word parā-vāc, and the single OM praised by Gītā and Kaṭha as Brahman with and without attributes, identical with Viṣṇu's own Kṣara-Akṣara pair. In the other, perishables are bodies and the imperishable the soul, as the Gītā teaches, so She authors both vessel and occupant for the unfolding of karma. In practice, sound OM slowly while feeling breath perish and awareness persist, honoring Her as letter and spirit, body and dweller together.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-146"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-758", title: "Sarvalokeśī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the ruler of all worlds. Sarva means all, loka worlds, and īśvarī their mistress, so every plane answers to Her scepter. The commentary expounds the seven vyāhṛtis of the Gāyatrī as seven levels of awareness from earth to truth-heaven, Brahmā's abode, understanding cosmology as psychology writ large: to rule worlds is to govern states of mind from dullness to illumination, satya crowning them as the highest pitch of consciousness. Sovereignty maps inward. In practice, review your day across levels, appetite, energy, sentiment, thought, and aspiration, and invite Her rule into the unruliest province first, until all inner worlds pay tribute.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-146"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-759", title: "Viśvadhāriṇī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who supports the universe. Viśva means the all, dhāriṇī the bearer, so the name compresses providence into two words: nothing hangs unsupported. The commentary adds nothing elaborate, as if brevity befitted a pillar, letting the image work directly on confidence and trust. Where other names divide Her labor into ruling, feeding, and dissolving, this one simply holds all things together without fanfare. In practice, when responsibility feels too heavy to carry alone, consciously transfer its weight upward for a moment, picturing the world and your share resting in vast hands, then resume labor as participation rather than solitary propping, grateful to be carried while you carry.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-146"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-760", title: "Trivargadātrī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who bestows the three goals of life. Trivarga means the triad of human ends, righteousness, prosperity, and pleasure, also called trayīmaya, while dātrī means giver, deliberately omitting the fourth end, liberation, already treated in the freedom names. The commentary refers the full quartet to the earlier giver-of-ends, keeping this triad for worldly completeness under divine supervision. To grant dharma first is to keep artha and kāma from corrupting. In practice, audit one desire against righteousness before pursuing gain or delight, asking Her to grant the three in order, and leave liberation to ripen as the orchard matures rather than forcing its fruit.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-146"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-761", title: "Subhagā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the seat of all prosperity. Su means excellent and bhaga spans fortune, welfare, dignity, beauty, and even the sun and Sāvitrī, while tradition greets a five-year-old girl as Subhagā, ripeness before bloom. The commentary reads Her as incarnate auspiciousness of the highest order, adding that if bhaga means sun, then daylight itself shines by Her allowance, a point prepared by the solar-disc names, and that even her passing thought conveys blessing. Favor here is ontological, not accidental. In practice, begin one undertaking by invoking Her fair name at dawn, straightening motive toward dignity and welfare, and proceed as one already favored rather than anxiously courting luck.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-146"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-762", title: "Tryambakā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who has three eyes. Tri means three and ambaka eyes, recalled as sun, moon, and fire, paralleling the three channels of kuṇḍalinī lore, lunar and solar currents with the central flame between them. The commentary adds a maternal reading: She mothers Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Rudra, the triple agents of emergence, as earlier womb-names intimated, so Her gaze creates as well as beholds. Three lights, one gaze, one Mother. In practice, balance the day's three fires by giving each its due, a little sunlight of action, a little moonlight of reflection, and a spark of aspiration, asking the three-eyed Mother to keep their proportion true through every season.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-146"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-763", title: "Triguṇātmikā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the essence of the three gunas. The guṇas are sattva, rajas, and tamas, nature's three strands whose interplay with spirit produces the world, and ātmikā means She is their very self when acting as Brahman with attributes. The commentary refers fuller treatment to the later guṇa-name, stressing that qualities belong to prakṛti in operation, while as attributeless Brahman She is declared guṇa-free and beyond them. Essence here means both substance and sovereign. In practice, label the strand dominating each hour, clarity, drive, or heaviness, without obeying it blindly, remembering that the Mother who wears the threads can also loosen them.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-146"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-764", title: "Svargāpavargadā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who bestows heaven and liberation. Svarga means the paradisal respite where meritorious souls taste delight before merit exhausts and return follows, as the Gītā warns, while apavarga means release from embodiment altogether, whether as living freedom or disembodied merger, attainable only through knowledge. The commentary is candid that subtle karmic seeds may survive even exalted wisdom and require further ripening in finer worlds, which is why ancestral rites and sesame offerings still matter to the tradition. She governs both rest-house and home. In practice, enjoy good fortune as wages rather than wages as home, using heaven-earned ease for study and charity so that respite shortens the road instead of delaying it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-147"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-765", title: "Śuddhā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the purest. Śuddhā means stainless, and the name holds Her up as purity itself rather than merely pure among others. The commentary links cleanliness directly to the previous gift: only the taintless can receive liberating knowledge, since stains are ignorance wearing moral dress and She carries none whatsoever. Purity here is not fastidiousness but unobstructed light shining through a clean lens. In practice, rinse one vessel of life today, a room, a sentence, a motive, until it shines, offering the act as imitation of Her, and notice how the mind clarifies in exact proportion to the cleansing, growing fit to receive what heaven longs to give.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-147"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-766", title: "Japāpuṣpanibhākṛtiḥ", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She whose body is like the hibiscus flower. Japā means the China rose, deep crimson, and the name says Her form glows with that color, exactly as the opening meditation verse envisions Her. The commentary needs only one line, since the image itself is the doctrine: redness is compassion made visible, intensity rather than ornament, love ripened into color. To see Her is to be warmed from within. In practice, place a red flower or simply its mental image before attention, breathing with its hue until devotion reddens into tenderness, and carry that color into the next encounter as quiet kindness that asks nothing in return.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-147"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-767", title: "Ojovatī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is full of vitality. Ojas means the subtlest bodily sap, the principle of warmth and vigor gathered in the spine and heart from food and perception, counted by physicians as the eighth tissue after the seven from lymph to seed, in two grades, higher and lower, and in measures from joined palms to eight drops. The commentary cites medical scripture enthroning ojas at conception itself, where the soul lodges after seed-union, proving Śakti present from life's first hour and resident in the heart thereafter. She is not added to life but is its sap. In practice, guard vitality like temple oil through wholesome food, sleep, continence, and measured speech, dedicating regained energy to remembrance rather than display.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-147"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-768", title: "Dyutidharā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is full of light and splendor; She who has an aura of light. Dyuti means brilliance and dharā its bearer, so the name crowns Her with the radiance streaming from initial and final I-awareness, the aham that comprehends the whole cosmos as the creative glitter of Śakti. The commentary treats illumination as selfhood shining outward rather than polish applied from without, since splendor is simply what being looks like when seen from within. No lamp is brought to Her because She is the lamp. In practice, steady attention on the simple sense of I-am until it brightens without objects, then let that inner daylight govern speech and choice, carrying one steady lamp into every room instead of hunting many.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-147"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-769", title: "Yajñarūpā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is in the form of sacrifice. Yajña means the fire rite where oblations ride mantra and flame to the gods, and Vedānta crowns Viṣṇu as its lord, even identifying the two, while the Gītā prefers silent mantra-repetition above all offerings and Viṣṇu's own thousand names enshrine sacrifice. The commentary therefore reads Her as Viṣṇu in function, then deepens rite into devotion itself: most offerings already favor Śakti, and the greatest oblation is the worship of one's own self. Ceremony rehearses self-giving. In practice, turn one routine act into yajña by dedicating its heat beforehand and reviewing its ashes afterward, letting work become worship without changing its outer shape.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-147"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-770", title: "Priyavratā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is fond of vows. Vrata means a resolved restraint, undertaken by solemn determination to please heaven, and priya means She delights in such binding. The commentary gives two reasons for Her pleasure even when vows target other deities: She is Brahman in both modes, and the whole universe is the joint display of spirit and nature, so every sincere promise lands at Her feet, a catholicity the Gītā confirms by stabilizing each devotee's faith in the chosen form. Fidelity matters more than address. In practice, take one small keepable vow for a fixed season, such as truth at cost or sweetness under provocation, and keep it as a lamp before Her rather than a chain around others.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-147"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-771", title: "Durārādhyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is difficult to worship. Dur means hard and ārādhya means to be approached in worship, so the name warns that nearness to Her is not cheap. The commentary locates the difficulty in mind and sense control: ritual without gathered attention yields no fruit, a point Saundarya Laharī presses by barring the uncontrolled from Her feet, echoed by a later name on the same theme. Steepness guards sanctity. In practice, train approach before approaching: steady the posture, slow the breath, gather the straying senses once daily, and only then address Her, accepting that the climb is part of the worship.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-148"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-772", title: "Durādharṣā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is difficult to control. Where the last name called Her hard to reach, this one calls Her impossible to coerce, yielding only to unstained devotion and scorning showmanship. The commentary summons the Gītā promise that leaf, flower, fruit, or water offered with love summons the Lord in person, stresses secluded worship repeated through the hymn, quotes the Bhāvanopaniṣad identifying the bliss of self as Lalitā, and recalls Durvāsā teaching that breath-control wedded to meditation merges the servant into Her. She is won, never taken. In practice, offer one humble thing daily in privacy without photograph or announcement, and let the secrecy itself prove the love unfeigned.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-148"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-773", title: "Pāṭalīkusumapriyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is fond of the pāṭali flower (the pale red trumpet flower). Pāṭalī blooms in red and white, and the name says She loves it because the colors wed Her scarlet energy to Śiva's white stillness in a single cup. The commentary pairs devotions accordingly: Śiva favors the bilva tree while Śakti favors the pāṭalī, so to offer this blossom is to honor the couple at once. Botany becomes theology. In practice, set before Her anything joining opposites harmoniously, two colors, two temperaments reconciled, or simply red resolve softened by white calm, and let the pairing remind you that union pleases Her more than partisanship.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-148"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-774", title: "Mahatī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is great; She who is in the form of mahatti (nārada's vīṇa). Mahat means the Great, arising in Sāṅkhya as the first stirring when spirit meets nature, composed of the three strands and unfolding as intellect, humanity's exclusive instrument for knowledge. The commentary hails Her as that cosmic intelligence manifest in every knower, cites the Upaniṣadic greater-than-the-greatest, and concludes that Śiva is realized only by Her blessing since intellect must first clear the road. Mahatī also strings Nārada's lute, making greatness musical. In practice, sharpen the instrument daily through study, reflection, and honest reasoning, then tune it by surrender, asking the Great to play wisdom through your small strings.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-148"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-775", title: "Merunilayā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who resides in the meru mountain. Meru is the fabled world-axis rising at the navel of the southern continent, already praised as Her central-peak seat, and nilayā means dwelling. The commentary reads the mountain fourfold: mythic Olympus, the three-dimensional Śrīcakra called Mahāmeru, the condensed Pañcadaśī reduced to nine seed-sounds with bindu, and the human spine up and down which kuṇḍalinī travels. To live on Meru is thus to sit at every center at once. In practice, straighten the spine in sitting, feel it as a mountain axis joining earth to sky, and place attention like a temple on its summit where She already dwells.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-148"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-776", title: "Mandārakusumapriyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is fond of the mandāra flowers. Mandāra is the coral tree, one of the five celestial trees of paradise, so its blossoms carry heaven's fragrance into worship. The commentary adds a Ganges hermitage with eleven holy pools that She loves, and a Buddhist memory of these same flowers raining from the sky while the Awakened One taught, as if truth itself perfumed the air. To cherish them is to keep paradise in mind. In practice, offer the rarest attention you can afford, an unhurried hour, a forgiven grudge, a choice bloom of conduct, trusting She weighs rarity of heart above rarity of petal.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-148"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-777", title: "Vīrārādhyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is worshipped by heroic persons. Vīra means the brave, defined here not by muscle but by realization: egoless, beyond opposites, fearless because established in Self, the courageous devotee. The commentary insists She is adored precisely by such warriors, since only those who have faced inward terror can stand unflinching before supreme majesty. Heroism is introverted before it is ever displayed on any field. In practice, perform one act requiring moral nerve, admitting fault, defending the weak, or keeping discipline unseen, and dedicate its trembling to Her, letting worship forge the very hero it honors and sending fear back to the shadows.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-149"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-778", title: "Virāḍrūpā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is in the form of the cosmic whole. Virāj or virāḍ means the fully manifested macrocosm, consciousness identified with the gross totality in waking life, the aggregate of quintuplicated elements and their effects as Śaṅkara's Pañcīkaraṇa defines it. The commentary unfolds four stations of awareness from unmanifest through sovereign and golden-womb to this all-pervading metamorphosis, warning that what eyes report is only a māyā-thinned slice of the whole, perfected by observation rather than theory. The universe is Her gross body. In practice, widen perception deliberately until street, sky, and stranger compose one figure, greeting the day as Her limbs in motion rather than as scenery around a separate self.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-149"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-779", title: "Virajā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is without rajas (desire and anger). Vi means without and rajas means stain, passion, and particulate impurity, so the name proclaims Her dustless. The commentary hears the great Upaniṣadic confession of freedom from sin and passion, recalls the purificatory fire-offering performed before renunciation that lends it its name, and draws the moral that to merge with stainless Brahman the seeker must grow stainless too, matching Her purity already praised. Like seeks like. In practice, submit breath, body, and intention to a daily cleansing, fasting one excess, forgiving one debt, clarifying one motive, so that approaching Her becomes resemblance rather than mere request.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-149"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-780", title: "Viśvatomukhī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who faces all directions. Viśvatas means on every side and mukhī faced, so no quarter lacks Her countenance. The commentary gathers Śvetāśvatara on all faces as His, the Gītā on the universal form worshipped in manifold celestial shapes, and the Puruṣa hymn on thousand-headed humanity, concluding that every living face is Hers and the verse reaffirms omnipresence in flesh and blood. Geography surrenders entirely to ubiquity. In practice, meet the next several faces you encounter as Hers in disguise, serving or at least acknowledging each with patience, until the exercise softens stranger into sanctuary and everywhere into temple, and no direction feels empty of Her regard.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-149"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-781", title: "Pratyagrūpā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the indwelling self. Pratyak means turned inward, so the name says She shows Herself to those who reverse the gaze. The commentary cites Kaṭha Upaniṣad on senses created outgoing by the Self-born Lord, blinding beings to the within, while another verse insists Brahman never yields to eyes but reveals Himself to minds made pure by constant remembrance, even though Nārāyaṇa pervades outside as well as inside. Omnipresence does not remove the need for introversion. In practice, briefly close the gates each day, drawing mind off sounds and sights onto breath and then onto witnessing itself, greeting the inward-facing Mother where She has waited unwatched.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-149"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-782", title: "Parākāśā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the transcendental ether (which is the material cause of the cosmic and individual bodies). Parākāśa means supreme space, likened to Brahman for vastness, purity, imperceptibility, and subtlety, the womb from which shine and rain descend. The commentary strings authorities like lamps: Brahma Sūtra on space as Brahman by indicatory mark, Chāndogya on all arising from space and returning there as highest goal, Taittirīya on bliss taught in the heart's hollow, and Kaṭha on Self everywhere as air between earth and heaven. Comparison tutors incomprehension. The hymn being mantra, it must be uttered plainly, never sung for show. In practice, rest attention in the heart's empty expanse, letting names and forms drift like clouds across Her sky.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-149"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-783", title: "Prāṇadā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the giver of life. Prāṇa means vital breath and dā giver, continuing the subtle-element litany where space gave way to breath, both life-giving and both under Her command like the sun and rain that space bestows. The commentary cites the Brahma Sūtra grounding that breath is Brahman, Chāndogya on things vanishing into breath and emerging from it, and Bṛhadāraṇyaka on knowers of the breath of breath, the taintless Self beyond subtle ether. To give breath is to lend being. In practice, watch one full cycle of breathing as Her gift arriving and departing, and return thanks by offering the next breath's ease to someone strained, circulating the loan.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-149"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-784", title: "Prāṇarūpiṇī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the nature of life. Where the last name called Her giver of breath, this one calls Her breath embodied, since only the full can overflow into sharing, a logic already used for liberation given and liberation incarnate. The commentary recalls Chāndogya instruction where fire teaches that breath and space, figured as two syllables, are one, illustrating that any pair rightly understood resolves into Brahman, so that knower and ignorant differ only by recognition. Gift and giver are a single exhalation. In practice, feel yourself breathed rather than breathing for a few rounds, letting the Mother as atmosphere move the lungs She lent, until gratitude replaces ownership of life.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-149"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-785", title: "Mārtāṇḍabhairavārādhyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is worshipped by mārtāṇḍabhairava. Mārtāṇḍa-bhairava is stationed at a far rampart of sacred Śrīpura, variously understood as the sun with his consort Shadow whom Durvāsā adores, or as a Vatuka attendant among sixty-four Bhairavas grouped under eight chiefs. The commentary favors the deeper reading where mārtāṇḍa means the flash and Bhairava the sudden blaze of realization, citing the Śiva Sūtra maxim that exertion flowering into lightning is Śiva, like a dark room flooded the instant lamps are lit after long study in twilight. She is adored by such illumined ones. In practice, honor both outer light and inner guardians, keeping steady study while praying for the flash that no study can schedule.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-150"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-786", title: "Mantriṇīnyastarājyadhūḥ", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who has entrusted Her regal responsibilities to Her mantriṇi. Mantriṇī means minister, here Śyāmalā also called royal Mātaṅgī, to whom the Empress delegates cosmic administration already glimpsed in the chariot and battle names. The commentary widens minister into mantra itself: the devotee steeped in potent syllables becomes mantrin, all letters originating from Her as mother of sounds and embodiment of every formula, while the Śiva Sūtras teach that contracted mind is mantra, sustained effort ripens it, and ripening flowers into non-duality. Government here is grammar mystically administered. In practice, take one mantra or sacred phrase into meditation with understanding rather than mechanics, letting mind and formula marry until duality quietly abdicates.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-150"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-787", title: "Tripureśī", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is the goddess of tripura. Tripura recalls the triple-fortified demon-city of gold, silver, and iron that Śiva burned, read inwardly as stages of ignorance, making His wife mistress of their ashes and queen of tantra adored by all its schools. The commentary adds a cosmic reading where the three cities are the worlds of the triple gods ringed around Meru where She dwells, over which She presides as chief. Either way She rules the triple. In practice, identify your own three forts, a cherished opinion, a defended habit, a walled wound, and invite Her flame to encircle them, consenting to lose fortification in exchange for horizon.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-150"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-788", title: "Jayatsenā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who has an army which is accustomed only to victory. Jaya means victory and senā army, so the name guarantees that Her forces know no defeat. The commentary moralizes the mythology: demons personify wickedness and gods embody virtues, so Her triumphant host means goodness structurally prevails, however late its parade may seem to impatient eyes. Victory is constitutional, not occasional, and no rout can ever be final against Her ranks. In practice, stop fighting one fault single-handed and summon Her regiment instead, pairing resolve with prayer, companionship, and wholesome routine, and advance expecting virtue to win because it fights on Her side and under Her banner.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-150"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-789", title: "Nistraiguṇyā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is devoid of the three guṇas. Nis means out and traiguṇya the triple-stranded realm, so the name lifts Her clear of sattva, rajas, and tamas that, wedded to nature, manufacture creation. The commentary sends the student back to the earlier negations and analyses where this freedom was established, keeping the point sharp by brevity. To exceed the strands is not to lack character but to author it. In practice, when a mood claims authorship of you, naming itself clarity, urgency, or heaviness, step back into the witness that watches threads twist, asking the strand-free Mother to keep you unentangled while you work within the weave.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-150"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-790", title: "Parāparā", category: "Sampradāya-satya", summary: "She who is both parā and aparā. Parā means supreme and aparā lower, and their hyphenated middle names the intermediate poise where Brahman begins manifesting, losing supremacy by degrees from full energy through mediocrity to displayed form. The commentary reproduces the earlier exposition: omnipresence demands both master and servant, giver and taker, creator and destroyer within one reality, as Śrī Rudram sings, while Upaniṣads divide superior cosmic knowing from inferior phenomenal lore, urging the former above Vedas and rites once Brahman is seen. Śiva is attributeless height, Śakti qualified descent, their union the complete. In practice, honor both ends of each pair you meet, sage and serpent alike as Brahman, serving each according to need without confusing masks with face.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-150"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-791", title: "Satyajñānānandarūpā", category: "Mūrta-saṃsāra", summary: "She who is truth, knowledge and bliss. Satya means reality, jñāna knowing, and ānanda delight, together composing Her form where the Taittirīya formula speaks of truth, knowledge, and infinity as Brahman. The commentary cherishes the substitution of bliss for infinity as maternal compassion: when delight arrives, extraneous infinities are forgotten, while Bṛhadāraṇyaka equates knowledge, bliss, and Brahman and Chāndogya calls the infinite itself happiness. Where the previous name mapped the qualified middle, this one seals the attributeless height. In practice, test each pursuit against the triad by asking whether it is true, whether it enlightens, and whether it delights without aftermath, keeping only what answers yes three times.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-151"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-792", title: "Sāmarasyaparāyaṇā", category: "Mūrta-saṃsāra", summary: "She who is immersed in a state of steady wisdom. Sāmarasya means perfect evenness and parāyaṇā devoted resort, and the name says Her evenness is equality with Śiva Himself, without whom neither can function after mutual austerities won their union. The commentary unfolds the metaphysics: self-luminous Śiva needs Śakti as mirror and inherent nature to behold Himself, while She needs Him lest activity lose ground, for bare reality without power would stiffen into inertia. Together they parent the worlds as consciousness wedded to bliss. In practice, marry stillness to energy in one day, sitting motionless before acting decisively, and check both extremes against Her equipoise until work itself tastes like wisdom.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-151"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-793", title: "Kapardinī", category: "Mūrta-saṃsāra", summary: "She who is the wife of kapardi (shiva, one with matted hair). Kaparda means braided and knotted locks, praised in Rudram litany, and kapardinī is the wife of Him who wears them, the ascetic crown of matted hair signifying mastery over time. The commentary adds Kālidāsa's brief hymn picturing Her own locks likewise knotted in Kālī mood, suggesting austerity shared rather than merely admired from afar. Grandeur here deliberately dresses as renunciation. In practice, simplify one outward vanity, hair, dress, or furnishing, wearing plainness as bridal ornament, and let the saved attention gather inward where the matted Lord waits with His equally adorned bride.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-151"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-794", title: "Kalāmālā", category: "Mūrta-saṃsāra", summary: "She who wears all sixty-four forms of art as a garland. Kalā means art and accomplishment, distinct from tantra as procedure, and mālā a garland, so the sixty-four disciplines become flowers on Her breast. The commentary plays on syllables where one letter hints the boundless and another bringing, reading limitless beauty into the very word, and insists that whatever touches Her grows boundless too. Culture is devotion made visible. In practice, learn or polish one art with worshipful patience, a verse, a tune, a drawing, a dish, laying progress at Her feet like adding a blossom to the garland She already wears.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-151"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-795", title: "Kāmadhuk", category: "Mūrta-saṃsāra", summary: "She who fulfills all desires. Kāmadhuk names the wish-cow that streams whatever is sought, echoing the earlier granter of wishes and the Triśatī parallels. The commentary conditions the bounty on purity of mind, citing Muṇḍaka counsel that only the cleansed obtain wished worlds, and marvels with Saundarya Laharī that She gives more than asked, and through Her feet rather than hands, as if humility heightened generosity. Abundance answers capacity. In practice, clarify the vessel before begging the milk: confess one mixed motive, steady one discipline, then place a single deep desire at Her feet, accepting increase, delay, or substitution as the Mother's better arithmetic.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-151"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-796", title: "Kāmarūpiṇī", category: "Mūrta-saṃsāra", summary: "She who has a desirable form. Kāma first of all means Śiva Himself, so the name confesses Her as His very shape, differing only in complexion, His crystal white transfused with Her deep red into sunrise orange where they sit together. The commentary also hears cosmic desire: Brahman wishing within to be born as many, She becomes the will-to-create, and by a third turn She assumes any form at pleasure, since desire and shape obey Her alike. Identity wears polarity as adornment. In practice, contemplate the divine couple as one dawn, letting reverence for distinction melt into recognition of unity, and ask that your own shapings of life serve creation rather than vanity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-151"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-797", title: "Kalānidhiḥ", category: "Mūrta-saṃsāra", summary: "She who is the treasurehouse of all arts. Where the garland displayed the arts, this name names their mine, the source from which all activity issues and by which the universe is administered, since Her form is activity itself according to the Śiva Sūtra. The commentary crowns action with knowledge, citing the Gītā promise that every deed without exception culminates in wisdom, so labor is ore destined for the smelter of understanding. To draw from Her is to work originally. In practice, trace one skill you exercise back to its spring, thanking the source before using the stream, and dedicate today's foremost act to ripening into insight rather than merely into result.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-152"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-798", title: "Kāvyakalā", category: "Mūrta-saṃsāra", summary: "She who is the art of poetry. Kāvya means the inspired utterance of seers and poets, prophetic and musical at once, and kalā here is that single art singled out from all, making Her the fountain and patroness of verse. The commentary observes how epics enshrine feminine eminence like Sītā at their moral center, then sketches the Rāmāyaṇa of Vālmīki in seven books and twenty-four thousand stanzas portraying the perfect sufferer, suggesting poetry's office is to house Her. Meter becomes mansion. In practice, read one noble stanza aloud slowly or compose a plain couplet of gratitude, offering rhythm itself as homage to Her who first inspired singing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-152"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-799", title: "Rasajñā", category: "Mūrta-saṃsāra", summary: "She who knows all the rasas. Rasa means sap, essence, flavor, and the relish of the heart, classically five devotional moods from peace through service, friendship, parenthood, and sweetness, or eight dramatic flavors from love to grace with contentment and fondness sometimes added. The commentary, following Saundarya Laharī, calls Her the finest of these savors, the connoisseur in whom every mood is perfectly tasted and judged. Feeling here becomes connoisseurship. In practice, notice which flavor colors your prayer today, whether awe, affection, or quietude, and offer that very taste consciously, asking the Knower of relish to refine it from raw reaction into seasoned devotion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-152"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-800", title: "Rasaśevadhiḥ", category: "Mūrta-saṃsāra", summary: "She who is the treasurehouse of rasa. Where the last name called Her knower of flavor, this one calls Her its inexhaustible store, the receptacle of sweetness itself. The commentary translates the Taittirīya saying that the Real is to be recognized as the honey in everything, teaching that whoever tastes this underlying sweetness knows the Self, since Brahman is the fountain of joy and joy realized ripens into bliss. Aesthetics resolves into metaphysics. In practice, pause over one ordinary sweetness, bread, light, friendship, or breath, tracing its delight backward to the common source, and rest briefly in the treasure-house rather than rushing past its door with the goods.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-152"] },
];
